Let me write a clean implementation:

```c
static int path_is_self_or_descendant(const char *dir, const char *path)
{
    struct stat dir_st, path_st;
    if (stat(dir, &dir_st) == 0 && stat(path, &path_st) == 0) {
        if (dir_st.st_dev == path_st.st_dev && dir_st.st_ino == path_st.st_ino)
            return 1;
    }

    size_t dlen = strlen(dir);
    if (strncmp(dir, path, dlen) != 0)
        return 0;
    if (dlen == strlen(path))
        return 1;  /* same path */
    if (path[dlen] == '/')
        return 1;  /* path is inside dir */
    return 0;
}
```

This handles:
- Same path (self-copy): `strncmp` matches and lengths equal → 1
- Path is inside dir: prefix matches and next char is `/` → 1
- Sibling (e.g., `dir2` when dir is `dir`): prefix matches but next char is not `/` → 0
- Inode-based detection: covers symlink aliases and hard links to the same directory

This is clean, doesn't use `realpath`, and correctly handles all the cases the spec requires. The inode check handles the "filesystem alias" case mentioned in the spec.

